<Project>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <!-- Warnings are errors in CI (CLAUDE.md convention); locally they stay warnings -->
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors Condition="'$(CI)' == 'true' or '$(TF_BUILD)' == 'true'">true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Package metadata, set once for every package rather than per project.

    Five packages whose versions can drift apart is a support problem: an inter-package
    dependency resolving to a mismatched version works on the machine that built it and
    fails on somebody else's. One property makes that impossible rather than unlikely.

    `PackageId` is deliberately absent — it defaults to the assembly name, which is
    already what each package should be called. The one exception sets it itself.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <!--
      The major tracks the UMBRACO major this package targets, not uBookIt's own
      breaking changes — see README.md's versioning note.

      Bumping this alone will FAIL THE TEST SUITE (not the build): VersionTruthTests
      reads this value and checks it against the documents that state uBookIt's
      version, so the prose has to follow. It matches those claims by the PHRASINGS it
      knows rather than by scanning for version-shaped text — a new document inventing a
      new way to say it is not covered, so add the phrasing there when you add one.
    -->
    <Version>17.0.0</Version>

    <Authors>Norwood Development</Authors>
    <Company>Norwood Development</Company>
    <Product>uBookIt</Product>
    <Copyright>Copyright © Norwood Development</Copyright>

    <PackageLicenseExpression>MIT</PackageLicenseExpression>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    <PackageTags>umbraco;umbraco-marketplace;booking;bookings;scheduling;reservations</PackageTags>

    <!--
      ============================================================================
      CORRECT THESE BEFORE THE FIRST PUSH TO A PUBLIC FEED. They are not yet right.
      ============================================================================

      Both URLs name a PRIVATE Azure DevOps organisation: a consumer following them
      from nuget.org or the Umbraco Marketplace reaches a sign-in wall, not a
      repository. The project is moving to a public GitHub home (Chris, 2026-09-14);
      these were left pointing at the current remote rather than at a guessed URL that
      does not exist yet.

      Why this is a publish-time gate rather than a merge-time one: nuget.org does not
      allow a pushed version's metadata to be edited. Getting this wrong is not a
      commit away from fixed — it costs a version number and leaves the bad listing
      permanently visible.

      Deliberately not guarded by a test: there is no in-repo signal for "about to
      publish", and a test asserting these are NOT the Azure URLs would fail today,
      when they are correctly the Azure URLs. The check that matters is a human one at
      push time, which is why it is stated here, at the point of use.
    -->
    <PackageProjectUrl>https://dev.azure.com/NorwoodDesignDev/uBookIt</PackageProjectUrl>
    <RepositoryUrl>https://dev.azure.com/NorwoodDesignDev/uBookIt/_git/uBookIt</RepositoryUrl>
    <RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>

    <!--
      Deterministic-ish niceties that cost nothing and help a consumer debug: the symbol
      package means a stack trace through uBookIt has line numbers.
    -->
    <IncludeSymbols>true</IncludeSymbols>
    <SymbolPackageFormat>snupkg</SymbolPackageFormat>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    One README, in every package, rather than a full one on the aggregate and a stub on
    each library.

    The design left this open and guessed at "full on the meta, pointer on the libraries".
    Rejected on apply for a reason this project keeps relearning: two documents saying
    overlapping things drift, and the drift is silent. Four identical copies are noise;
    two versions of the truth are a defect waiting to happen.

    Guarded on IsPackable so it is not attached to test projects, and `Condition="Exists"`
    so a partial checkout does not fail the build with a packaging error.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(IsPackable)' != 'false' AND Exists('$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)README.md')">
    <None Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" Visible="false" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
